#
# The kernel the 1bit OS boots (docs/kernel.md). Until this file, its tag was named only in prose, and its config of
# record existed only in /boot on the reference box, so a kernel bump started with an archaeology step. Now the
# tag, the commit the tag points at, the release string `uname -r` prints and the config it was built from are all
# here. A kernel bump is a change to this file plus the new os/kernel/config-<release> (Refs Gantt "Kernel 7.4
# readiness").
#
# sha= is the COMMIT `v7.4-rc1` points at (refs/tags/v7.4-rc1^{}); tag_object= is the annotated tag object itself.
# config_sha256 is the sha256 of os/kernel/config-<release>, byte for byte the box's /boot/config-<release>
# (read 2026-11-13; built 2026-11-04). tests/kernel_pin_test.sh checks the two agree.
# compiler= is what built it: gcc, per CONFIG_CC_VERSION_TEXT. The docs' LLVM=1 recipe is not what built this
# image (docs/kernel.md, "config of record"); which one should change is a kernel-lane decision.
source=https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git
tag=v7.4-rc1
sha=6628e7d4c6b25c8d18f1345e3a20d21829aae7e4
tag_object=bc71431221f5ba8bb33918399552636e059c2c45
release=7.4.0-rc1-1bit
config=os/kernel/config-7.4.0-rc1-1bit
config_sha256=85749b7c0bb21223914ac454c5b895a0180e83fe0688474358cd3b42e99777a2
compiler=gcc (Ubuntu 15.2.0-16ubuntu1) 15.2.0
